Issues
1. sameSite: 'strict' will likely break the redirect flow
If /dashboard is on the same site as the OAuth callback, this is fine. But if the frontend is on a different origin (e.g., app.example.com calling an API on api.example.com), a strict cookie won't be sent on the redirect navigation from Google, or on subsequent cross-site requests from the frontend to the API. Use lax for this kind of top-level redirect flow unless you've confirmed everything is same-site.
2. Hardcoded redirect to /dashboard
This works only if your frontend is served from the same host as this Express app. If your frontend lives elsewhere, you need res.redirect(process.env.CLIENT_URL + '/dashboard') or similar — otherwise Google will redirect back to your API server, not your app.
3. No validation that req.user exists
If passport.authenticate somehow calls next() without setting req.user (edge case, but possible with custom strategies or misconfiguration), student._id will throw inside the try/catch — caught, but you'll get a generic 500 with no useful log. Add a quick guard and log the actual error:
javascriptasync (req, res) => {
    try {
        const student = req.user;
        if (!student) {
            return res.status(401).json({ success: false, message: 'Authentication failed' });
        }

        const token = jwt.sign(
            { id: student._id, role: student.role, email: student.email },
            process.env.JWT_SECRET_KEY,
            { expiresIn: '7d' }
        );

        res.cookie('idCard', token, {
            httpOnly: true,
            secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production',
            sameSite: 'lax', // 'strict' will break this cross-origin redirect
            maxAge: 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000
        });

        return res.redirect(`${process.env.CLIENT_URL}/dashboard`);

    } catch (error) {
        console.error('Google OAuth callback error:', error);
        return res.status(500).json({
            success: false,
            message: 'Google login failed'
        });
    }
}
4. Failed auth still just redirects to /login, no error context
If you want the frontend to show why login failed (denied access, no email scope, etc.), consider a custom callback instead of relying on failureRedirect, so you can pass an error reason as a query param.
5. Minor: cookie name idCard is unusual naming for a JWT
Not a bug, but something like authToken or sessionToken is more conventional and easier for teammates to recognize at a glance.
What's good

httpOnly + secure (conditionally) + JWT expiry matching cookie maxAge — solid baseline.
session: false is correct for a stateless JWT-based flow.
try/catch around the token-signing logic protects against crashes.

