What sameSite does
It tells the browser: "only attach this cookie to requests that come from [this context]." It's a defense against CSRF attacks. There are three values:
strict
The cookie is sent only when the request originates from the same site the cookie was set on. Even clicking a link from an external site won't include the cookie on the very first request.
lax (the middle ground, and the default in modern browsers if unspecified)
The cookie is sent on same-site requests, plus on top-level navigations from another site — like clicking a link, or being redirected — as long as it's a safe method (GET). It's blocked on cross-site subrequests like fetch, images, iframes.
none
Cookie is sent on all requests, cross-site included. Requires secure: true (HTTPS only).
Why this matters for your flow
Walk through what actually happens in your OAuth flow:

User is on your frontend, clicks "Login with Google"
Browser navigates to Google (accounts.google.com) — a different origin
User approves, Google redirects the browser back to yourapi.com/auth/google/callback
Your server sets the idCard cookie and redirects again to /dashboard

Step 3 is the critical one. That redirect from accounts.google.com → yourapi.com is a cross-site, top-level navigation. It's not your site initiating it — Google's server is.

With strict: the cookie you're setting would actually still get set fine here (you're setting it, not reading it, on your own domain) — but the risk is more on the next request. If your dashboard page then needs to read that cookie via a fetch/XHR call from a different subdomain or origin, strict will silently drop it, and you'll get confusing 401s that look like the login didn't work.
With lax: that top-level redirect and subsequent same-origin-ish navigation works normally, since lax allows cookies on top-level GET navigations from cross-site contexts.

So the practical symptom of using strict in an OAuth flow is: login appears to succeed, cookie gets set, but the very next authenticated request fails to include the cookie — especially if your frontend and API are on different subdomains or ports (very common in dev, e.g. localhost:3000 vs localhost:5000).
